// _capability.memql -- authoring reference skeleton for the `capability`
// construct (construct-invocation epic memql#2322, Story 5 / memql#2325).
//
// This file is REFERENCE-ONLY. Files and directories under dsl/_reference/
// are NOT loaded by the engine (the loaders skip underscore-prefixed
// paths); they document each construct's authoring shape for humans + Sense.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// What a `capability` is
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// A surface-backed external capability VERB. It is declared like a typed,
// side-effect-classified `builtin` with NO body -- the implementation is
// supplied by the runtime surface, not the DSL.
//
//   - Name is namespaced/DOTTED. The leading segment is the namespace, one
//     of the five-namespace closed vocabulary:
//       fs.*           filesystem               (fs.readFile, fs.writeFile)
//       shell.*        process execution        (shell.exec, shell.script)
//       http.*         outbound HTTP            (http.get, http.post)
//       integration.*  a named integration      (integration.<domain>.<fn>)
//       mcp.*          an MCP tool invocation   (mcp.<tool>)
//
//   - @sideEffect("read"|"write"|"exec") lives HERE. This is the
//     UNSPOOFABLE side-effect classification (ADR §7): an authored or
//     planner-generated action that invokes the capability cannot claim a
//     softer class than the capability declares.
//
//   - @description(...) documents the verb; it is also the planner's
//     embedding source.
//
//   - The body is an OPTIONAL `args { ... }` input schema, using the same
//     args-block grammar as action / builtin / file-top args. There is NO
//     `body { ... }` block -- a `body` is the procedural marker and a
//     capability is never procedural.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Declaration shape
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
@sideEffect("write")
@description("Create a git tag + GitHub release for a version.")
capability integration.github.tagRelease {
  args {
    repo string @required
    tag  string @required
  }
}

// A read capability with a single arg.
@sideEffect("read")
@description("Read a file from the local filesystem surface.")
capability fs.readFile {
  args {
    path string @required
  }
}

// An exec capability may take no args at all (args block omitted).
@sideEffect("exec")
@description("Run a shell script on the host surface.")
capability shell.script { }

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Importing + calling (verb-level)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Capabilities are imported at the VERB level, consistent with every other
// `use`. The import path is `capabilities.<namespace-path>.{ verbNames }`:
//
//     use capabilities.integration.github.{ tagRelease }
//     use capabilities.fs.{ readFile }
//
// and invoked via the call form (invocation grammar lands in Story 2):
//
//     capability tagRelease({ repo: args.repo, tag: args.tag })
//
// The action rewrite (Story 4) is what binds a capability call into an
// authored `action`. This story is purely the DECLARATION + import + the
// registry/cross-ref plumbing that lets a declared+imported capability
// validate.
